WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.664
Hi, I'm Ferrule!

00:00:02.664 --> 00:00:06.160
Bonsai 2 now comes in two, four and nine billion parameters.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:09.657
Here's where the bytes go when a ternary model gets small.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.590
Every weight matrix is still minus one, zero or plus one.

00:00:14.590 --> 00:00:18.080
But at two billion, the vocabulary table alone is over a

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:19.349
quarter of the model.

00:00:20.842 --> 00:00:24.266
The transformer blocks are about seventy percent of the file, packed five

00:00:24.266 --> 00:00:25.407
trits to a byte.

00:00:25.827 --> 00:00:28.204
The vocabulary table is a quarter of it.

00:00:28.204 --> 00:00:32.068
It doubles as the output head, so every token reads it in full.

00:00:32.488 --> 00:00:36.249
Norms and the state path stay in full precision, and the tokenizer

00:00:36.249 --> 00:00:37.503
rides in the header.

00:00:37.503 --> 00:00:39.070
Neither shrinks with the model.

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:42.983
That's why the small files land above the format's one point seven

00:00:42.983 --> 00:00:44.148
five bits per weight.

00:00:45.692 --> 00:00:47.750
The nine billion model ships twice.

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:51.866
One pack gives each trit a two-bit slot: two point four two

00:00:51.866 --> 00:00:53.238
gigabytes, the repost's number.

00:00:53.658 --> 00:00:57.161
The other packs five trits to a byte: two gigabytes, the number

00:00:57.161 --> 00:00:58.329
in the launch post.

00:00:58.329 --> 00:00:59.496
Same weights, different layout.

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:04.055
Decoding one token at a time is a memory problem.

00:01:04.475 --> 00:01:06.783
Each token streams every weight matrix once.

00:01:07.716 --> 00:01:10.178
So speed is just how fast the chip reads memory.

00:01:10.957 --> 00:01:14.848
Tokens per second times bytes gives the bandwidth a phone really used.

00:01:16.508 --> 00:01:18.701
The whitepaper measured an iPhone seventeen.

00:01:18.701 --> 00:01:23.087
The launch post quotes an iPhone eighteen, faster than the bandwidth math

00:01:23.087 --> 00:01:25.280
expects, with no method behind it.

00:01:26.558 --> 00:01:30.373
So at small sizes, the vocabulary and the packing decide the file

00:01:30.373 --> 00:01:31.644
size, not the trits.

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:37.163
To recap: a big vocabulary, two packings of the same weights, and

00:01:37.163 --> 00:01:39.044
speed set by bytes per token.

00:01:39.464 --> 00:01:41.564
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:41.564 --> 00:01:42.164
I'm Ferrule.

00:01:42.164 --> 00:01:42.464
Bye!
